{"name":"Chameleon","hex":"#cedaac","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chameleon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small to mid-size reptile, of the family Chamaeleonidae, and one of the best known lizard families able to change color and project its long tongue.","A person with inconstant behavior; one able to quickly adjust to new circumstances."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That changes or modifies its color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chameleon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chameleon","blocks":[{"text":"Chameleons are a distinctive and highly specialized clade of Old World lizards with 200 species described as of June 2015. The members of this family are best known for their distinct range of colours, being capable of colour-shifting camouflage. The large number of species in the family exhibit considerable variability in their capacity to change colour. For some, it is more of a shift of brightness ; for others, a plethora of colour-combinations can be seen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Panther_Chameleon_738367_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Panther_Chameleon_738367_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chameleon","wikidata":"Q37686"},{"title":"Chameleon (character)","blocks":[{"text":"The Chameleon is a supervillain appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. Created by writer Stan Lee and artist Steve Ditko, the character first appeared in the debut issue of The Amazing Spider-Man in March 1963 as the first super-criminal to face Spider-Man. The Chameleon has since endured as one of Spider-Man's recurring enemies, belonging to the collective of adversaries that make up his rogues gallery."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/43/Dmitri_Smerdyakov_as_The_Chameleon_%282021%29.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chameleon_(character)","wikidata":"Q521305"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Verbreitung_von_Cham%C3%A4leons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Verbreitung_von_Cham%C3%A4leons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVerbreitung_von_Cham%C3%A4leons.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg/500px-Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg/1280px-Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg/500px-Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chameleons' eye coordination","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChameleons'_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg/500px-Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg/1280px-Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg/500px-Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mughal era painting of a chameleon by Ustad Mansur","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUstad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png/500px-Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png/500px-Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Colour change and iridophore types in panther chameleons: \n(a) Reversible colour of two males, one relaxed (m1), and one excited (m2). During excitation, background skin shifts from the baseline state (green) to yellow/orange, and both vertical bars and horizontal mid-body stripe shift from blue (m1) to whitish (m2).\n\n(b) Red dots show change in the CIE chromaticity chart of a male with green skin. white line: optical response in numerical simulations using a face-centred cubic lattice of guanine crystals with lattice parameter indicated with black arrows.\n(c) Haematoxylin and eosin staining of a cross-section of white skin showing the epidermis and the two thick layers of iridophores.\n(d) TEM images of guanine nanocrystals in S-iridophores in the excited state and three-dimensional model of an FCC lattice (shown in two orientations).\n(e) TEM image of guanine nanocrystals in D-iridophores.[18]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg/500px-Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_